# iCanHazMusic – notes for coding agents
A native macOS music player (SwiftUI + AppKit, Swift package, minimal dependencies).
Target: macOS 15.4+, Swift tools 5.10 (Swift 5 language mode).

This file is a map plus the few invariants that are easy to break. Everything else is in the code (doc comments are there): read it rather than relying on this file.

## Layout
| Path | What |
|---|---|
| `src/` | The app (single SwiftPM executable target `iCanHazMusic`). |
| `tests/` | Swift Testing suite, see [Tests](#tests). |
| `vendor/` | WavPack and Monkey's Audio decoders (targets `CWavPack`, `CMonkeysAudio`). **Upstream files are never edited**, they are copied by `vendor/update.sh`; our code is in `vendor/monkeys-audio/shim/`. See `vendor/README.md`. |
| `dev-docs/` | Background notes, read the relevant one before touching that area: `tag-parsing.md` (macOS tag APIs, why the reader is built the way it is), `playback.md` (why `AVAudioEngine` and not `AVPlayer`, engine internals, state restore), `lyrics.md` (lyrics sources, LRCLIB), `cue.md` (how CUE sheets are read, stored and played), `extra-formats-support.md` (how WavPack/APE are supported and what is left). |
| `build.sh` | Builds the `.app` bundle in `dist/`. |
| `Info.plist`, `res/` | Bundle metadata and resources copied into the bundle by `build.sh`. |

`src/` has three parts: `App/` (entry point, `AppDelegate`, `AppTermination`), `Core/` (logic, grouped by feature: `Config`, `Playlists`, `Import`, `Tags`, `Playback`, `Integrations`, ...) and `UI/` (views and AppKit glue, grouped the same way). One view per file.

- Main entry points: `ConfigStore` (settings), `PlaylistStore` (all playlists, kept in memory), `ImportSession` (files -> tags -> albums), `PlaybackEngine` (audio) and `PlaybackState` (playlist logic on top of the engine), `PlaybackListener` (Last.fm, lyrics, notifications and the waveform follow playback through it).

## Worth knowing before you change things
**General**
- Last.fm credentials are **not in the sources**: `build.sh` takes them from `.env` and writes them (obfuscated) into the bundle's `Info.plist`. `build.sh` reads `LastFMCredentials.obfuscationKey` from its line in `LastFMAPI.swift`, keep that format. Never put credentials into sources or tests.
- Notifications and self-update need a **signed bundle** (`ICHM_SIGNING_IDENTITY`; ad-hoc without it). Plain `swift build` gives an unbundled executable where they are unavailable.
- **The app quits itself only through `AppTermination.request(reason:)`**, never call `terminate` elsewhere: from async main-actor code it hangs the app while something plays. It's also ignored while a sheet is attached, so dismiss sheets first.

**Playlists and tracks**
- Refer to tracks by `TrackID` wherever a reference must outlive a playlist edit; `(album, track)` positions and row numbers are only valid for one `Playlist` instance.
- A CUE track has the pseudo path `/path/album.cue:N` and a `cue` object, always together. Compare playback sources, never URLs (`dev-docs/cue.md`).
- Edits that replace the active content go through `PlaylistStore` and tell `PlaybackState.playlistWasReplaced`, so the playing track is found again by ID.

**Playback**
- `AVAudioEngine`, **not `AVPlayer`** (`dev-docs/playback.md`).
- **Files are read through a `PCMSource`** (`Core/Playback/Sources`, picked by `PCMSources.open` from the extension), never `AVAudioFile` directly: `AVAudioFileSource` for what macOS opens, `WavPackSource` and `APESource` (the vendored decoders) for `.wv` and `.ape`, which macOS can't open at all. Only those two import `CWavPack`/`CMonkeysAudio`; tags of these formats (APEv2, `ApeTag`) are plain Swift. A source's `codec` text and the one made at import (`TagReadResult.codec`) must be the same.
- **A track counts as started only when its audio does** (the engine's `.started` event): listeners, logs and `trackDidEnd` wait for it, a file that can't be played is never announced.
- **A file that doesn't answer (network share gone) must never stall the feeder** or the main thread.
- Tracks without tags are never looked up on LRCLIB or sent to Last.fm.
- `AppKit` callbacks from the system (media keys, `MPRemoteCommandCenter`) arrive on their own queue: build their closures in `nonisolated` helpers (isolated closures trap).

**Config and caches**
- Change settings only through `ConfigStore.shared.update { ... }`; limits for validation live in `ConfigLimits.swift`.
- Every network request is built with `URLRequest(appURL:timeout:)` (sets the `User-Agent`).
- Bump `Waveform.formatVersion` when the waveform encoding or layout changes. The app never prunes `waveforms.sqlite`.

**UI**
- **UI may depend on Core, never the other way round.** Core is Foundation only (plus UI-less system frameworks such as CryptoKit, Carbon, AudioToolbox): never import SwiftUI/AppKit there. Whatever needs AppKit (opening URLs, alerts) is handed to Core as a closure.
- **What is hidden is not done:** a hidden Now Playing component (or the whole block) must not cost work (art loading, waveform analysis). Whoever acts on that registers with `addVisibilityObserver`.
- The playlist selector and the Now Playing block are custom `SidePanel`s in `PlayerArea`, not `NavigationSplitView`/inspector, on purpose.
- Keyboard handling is one local `NSEvent` monitor in `VirtualPlaylistView`, no `focusable`/`@FocusState` on purpose; the rows' context menu (an `NSMenu`) is opened from it too. The monitor outlives the view struct: **never capture view values in it**.
- **Playlist rows must stay light**: scrolling mounts them constantly. No gestures, hover or context menus per row (they are handled once for the whole content), no blocking lookups in a row's `body` (e.g. `LyricsStore.contains` answers from memory, covers are loaded asynchronously by `CoverCache`).
- Drag reordering and scrolling in `VirtualPlaylistView` are fiddly and not covered by tests: read the comments before changing them.
- `MigrationActions.askAtLaunch()` runs before any singleton touches the working directory; `NSApp` doesn't exist yet there, use `NSApplication.shared`.

## Runtime data
Everything lives in `~/Library/Application Support/iCanHazMusic` (`AppPaths`). Pass `--workdir /some/dir` to use another directory.
- `app.log` (current run; earlier ones are rotated), `config.json` (keys and defaults in `Config.swift`), `playlists/{name}.json` (one file per playlist), `.cache/*.sqlite` (covers, lyrics, waveforms; safe to delete).

This is the user's real dev data. When testing, prefer seeding/inspecting the files over deleting them, and leave the directory in a sane state afterwards.

## Building and running
- Full dev build + launch: `./build.sh` (clean build, then **runs the app in the foreground** with `--workdir ~/Library/Application Support/iCanHazMusic-dev`, so the real data stays untouched). It blocks until the app quits: don't use it from an agent, the user normally runs it.
- Compile check only: `swift build -c debug --arch arm64`.
- Fast typecheck without SwiftPM (needs the module maps SwiftPM generated for the C targets, i.e. one earlier `swift build`): `swiftc -typecheck -parse-as-library -target arm64-apple-macos15.4 -Xcc -fmodule-map-file=.build/arm64-apple-macosx/debug/CWavPack.build/module.modulemap -Xcc -fmodule-map-file=.build/arm64-apple-macosx/debug/CMonkeysAudio.build/module.modulemap $(find src -name '*.swift')`.
- Tests only: `./build.sh test` (`swift test`). `dev-release` and `release` run it too and abort on failure; plain `./build.sh` skips it.
- Scratch experiments go to `tmp/` (git-ignored; never use `dist/`, `build.sh` wipes it); delete them afterwards.

## Tests
`swift test` runs the Swift Testing suite in `tests/` (`@testable import iCanHazMusic`). All suites are nested in `AllTests` and run serialized on the main actor; mark new suites `@MainActor @Suite(...)` inside `extension AllTests`.

- Audio/image fixtures are committed (`tests/fixtures`); `tests/prepare-fixtures.sh` is their recipe (needs `ffmpeg`, `mac`, `wavpack` and `wvtag`, output is byte-identical with the same tool versions). Add or change a fixture there, document it there, and regenerate only for that: a new ffmpeg rewrites every encoded file. `.gitignore` ignores `*.mp3` but not under `tests/fixtures`.
- Tests never touch the real working directory: stores and services take their locations/dependencies as parameters, tests use `TempDir` (`tests/Support/Fixtures.swift`). **Don't touch `.shared` singletons from tests.**
- Playback tests run the engine **offline** (`PlaybackEngine(output: .offline(sampleRate:))`, driven by `EngineRig` in `tests/Support`); `PlaybackState(store:engine:tickInterval:)` with `tickInterval: nil` and a manual `tick()`.
- Not covered: the real output device and the SwiftUI/AppKit layer.
- A **crash** in the test process (failed `precondition`, force-unwrapped nil, ObjC exception) looks like a **hang** of `swift test`: the backtracer symbolicates for minutes. Run with `SWIFT_BACKTRACE=enable=no` to fail fast, and wrap long runs in `timeout`. Test helpers should report problems with `#expect`/return values, not trap.

## Rules & Conventions
- Keep config validation limits in `ConfigLimits.swift` (values outside them are reset to defaults) and layout constants in `Layout.swift`.
- Playlist name rules and file operations live in `PlaylistStore`; UI flows (prompt, confirm, error) in `PlaylistActions`.
- Use `Log.info` / `Log.error` (timestamp + `[iCHM]` prefix, stdout and `app.log`) rather than bare `print`. Log whatever seems important but not overly noisy.
- Dialogs are `NSAlert` sheets on the main window (`Dialogs`), not SwiftUI alerts.
- There should be no compilation warnings. If you see one, fix it even if it's not from the recent changes.
- Prefer the included tools for reading, writing and editing the files.
- Update `.rules` only with high-level information, quirks and things that are useful to know before digging into the project, no need to add here every little thing that could be easily inferred from the code and its comments.
